WEBVTT

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.420
Right, a picture 1st. Think of a storage
room, numbered bins, and a clerk who looks

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:11.920
after them.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.880
You ask for somewhere to put something.
They find an empty bin, write the number

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.460
in their book, and hand you that number on
a note.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.080
When you're done, you tell them, and they
cross it out of their book again.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:24.940
Let's go straight into C.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.200
The note is a pointer variable. We'll call
it scores.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:34.260
The number on it is an address, and you
never make up one. Malloc, memory

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.860
allocation, gives it to you.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.920
The bin is the block, so the space you
got.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.110
And the clerk's book is the allocator's
bookkeeping.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.640
Notice, the book isn't in the picture, and
there's no name in C for it either.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.200
Handing, handing the back--

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:56.040
the bin back is what the function free
does, and it changes the book. It doesn't

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.020
change your note, however. That still says
4, one, 2, and nothing in C stops you

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.330
using it. Most of the bugs that you will
encounter

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.500
are the clerk disagreeing about who's got
4, one, 2 with you.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.100
the kernel hands those out already zeroed.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.160
That's luck, not a promise.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.340
And here are the same 16 bytes straight
after free. Glibc has written its own

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.219
bookkeeping into the 1st eight, scrambled,
and it's derived from this block's own

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:20.520
address.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.900
The 2nd eight are a key it stamps in so it
can spot you freeing the same block

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:26.300
twice.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.820
So reading scores index zero after freeing
reads 4 bytes of that.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.420
On this machine, it's a big negative
number, different every run because the

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.000
address is randomized. Elsewhere, it might
be positive or the same every time.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.661
and the sanitizer names it. Stack use
after return.

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:45.121
Bug 2. Let's look at the code as well.

00:01:48.981 --> 00:01:53.111
I've moved the print down below the free,
so I'm reading through the pointer after

00:01:53.111 --> 00:01:54.121
handing back the blog.

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:56.031
Again, let's compile

00:01:57.981 --> 00:01:59.201
and execute.

00:02:02.681 --> 00:02:03.421
Not 42.

00:02:04.481 --> 00:02:05.281
Let's run it again.

00:02:09.041 --> 00:02:13.780
Different score. And you know why. We
looked at the bytes. That's glibc's

00:02:13.781 --> 00:02:17.621
scrambled link field, and I'm reading 4
bytes of it as an int.

00:02:18.421 --> 00:02:20.161
Let's sanitize it

00:02:21.861 --> 00:02:22.681
and run that again.

00:02:25.661 --> 00:02:28.000
And the sanitizer gives you the history.
Used here, freed here, allocated here.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.041
line 7, and nothing ever gave them back.

00:02:31.281 --> 00:02:33.861
Well, all 3 bugs are kind of the same
mistake.

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:40.381
When the program's correct, the pointer
sits inside the lifetime of what it points

00:02:40.381 --> 00:02:46.301
at. Dangling pointer and use after free,
the pointer outlives the memory. A leak is

00:02:46.301 --> 00:02:47.161
that flipped over.

00:02:47.761 --> 00:02:49.321
The memory outlives every pointer.

00:02:50.281 --> 00:02:53.500
So it's the one question asked 3 ways. Do
those bars line up?
